From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 02:53:21 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: describe the FRITZ!Box 7590 WAN port

The board's fifth Ethernet port, on the second GSWIP instance.

intel,dp-port-id = <15> is forced rather than chosen: DP_F_FAST_ETH_WAN
drives the datapath allocator over a range whose start and end are both
PMAC_ETH_WAN_ID, so the WAN class can only land on datapath port 15. The
driver now rejects a lantiq,wan on any other port rather than quietly
handing it the wrong PMAC template.

lantiq,wan is not redundant with the port id. It is what selects the PMAC
template, the two Ethernet classes differ only in the redirect bit, and
nothing else on the node implies it. It is also the property the vendor's
own board glue reads.

Everything downstream follows from those two: the CQM resolves dequeue
port 19 (which is also the TMU egress port), TMU queue 28, scheduler
block 12 and DMA1TX channel 15 out of its ported vendor tables. The
controller is the part worth noticing - every LAN port drains via DMA2TX
and this one does not.

The fifth GPHY is deliberately still absent, pending the reset-bit-31
question, and the consequence is worse than "WAN stays dark" so both
comments now say it outright: with no firmware the WAN PHY does not
answer MDIO, no phy_device is created, and phylink_of_phy_connect returns
-ENODEV. That is not deferred and not confined to this port - probe
requires every declared port to come up, so it rolls back the four
working LAN netdevs too and the board ends up with no ethernet at all.
The fifth GPHY must land before this board is booted.

dtc: all three boards build; the 7560 and easy350_anywan DTBs are
byte-identical to before this commit.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index e6ba85f127e1..dc1eeaa54a41 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -172,7 +172,14 @@ partition@1500000 {
  * and they reverted to ROM.
  *
  * Adding the fifth PHY is one appended entry in each of these three
- * properties. It lands with the WAN port it serves.
+ * properties: phy_id 0x0F, reset-name "phyF", and its reset cell.
+ *
+ * ⚠ Until it is added THIS BOARD HAS NO ETHERNET AT ALL, not merely no WAN.
+ * The WAN port declared above hard-requires its PHY: with no firmware the PHY
+ * does not answer MDIO, phylink_of_phy_connect returns -ENODEV rather than
+ * deferring, and the driver requires every declared port to come up, so the
+ * four working LAN netdevs are rolled back with it. Land the fifth GPHY
+ * before booting this board.
  */
 &gphy_fw {
 	phy_id = <0x02 0x03 0x04 0x05>;
@@ -218,3 +225,40 @@ gphy_wan: ethernet-phy@1 {
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * The WAN port, on the second GSWIP instance.
+ *
+ * intel,dp-port-id = <15> is not a free choice: DP_F_FAST_ETH_WAN drives the
+ * datapath allocator over a range whose start and end are both
+ * PMAC_ETH_WAN_ID, so the WAN class can only ever land on datapath port 15.
+ * The driver rejects a lantiq,wan on any other port rather than quietly
+ * giving it the wrong PMAC template.
+ *
+ * lantiq,wan is what selects that template. It is not redundant with the port
+ * id: the two Ethernet classes differ only in the PMAC redirect bit, which no
+ * address or phy property implies, and it is the property the vendor's own
+ * board glue reads.
+ *
+ * From here the CQM resolves the rest out of its ported vendor tables --
+ * dequeue port 19 (which is also the TMU egress port), TMU queue 28,
+ * scheduler block 12, DMA1TX channel 15. Note the controller: every LAN port
+ * egresses via DMA2TX, this one does not.
+ *
+ * ⚠ ORDERING: this port cannot come up until the fifth GPHY is added to
+ * &gphy_fw below. Without its firmware the WAN PHY does not answer MDIO, no
+ * phy_device is created for gphy_wan, and phylink_of_phy_connect returns
+ * -ENODEV -- which is NOT deferred and NOT confined to this port. Probe
+ * requires every declared port to come up, so the failure rolls back the four
+ * working LAN netdevs with it and the board ends up with no ethernet at all.
+ * Adding the fifth GPHY is three appended entries; see the comment on
+ * &gphy_fw.
+ */
+&intel_xrx500_net {
+	port@4 {
+		reg = <4>;
+		intel,dp-port-id = <15>;
+		lantiq,wan;
+		phy-handle = <&gphy_wan>;
+	};
+};
